// Rep Max questions: History (OCR J411 BF), GCSE. One question per line, no commas between lines.
// Fields: id (unique), s (subject), t (topic id from spec.json), lv (1 warm-up, 2 working set, 3 hardest; Higher-only topics are 3),
// q (question; maths between $ signs, backslashes doubled), code (optional), o (four options), a (index 0-3 of the right one), x (explanation).
// Written by AI (Sonnet) and checked independently by AI (Opus), 15 Nov 2026; logs in the library's provenance folder.
{"id":"ghi-001","s":"history","t":"ph-1","lv":1,"q":"Many medieval people believed disease was spread by 'miasma'. What was miasma?","o":["Tiny living things in water","A punishment sent by the king","Poison put in wells by foreigners","Foul-smelling air from rotting matter"],"a":3,"x":"Medieval people thought bad smells carried disease. Germ theory was not developed until the 1860s, so towns tried to remove smells from rubbish, sewage and slaughterhouses.","w":["Mixes it up with germ theory, which only came in the 1860s.","Confuses it with 'disease as punishment'; miasma was about bad air.","Mixes it up with well-poisoning rumours in the Black Death; miasma was smelly air.",""]}
{"id":"ghi-002","s":"history","t":"ph-1","lv":2,"q":"During the Black Death in 1348-49, what did flagellants do?","o":["Shut sick people inside their homes","Burned the clothes of the dead","Whipped themselves in public to ask God to end the plague","Left towns to live in the countryside"],"a":2,"x":"Flagellants believed the plague was God's punishment and that suffering publicly would win forgiveness. Their processions spread from Europe, and they sometimes spread disease.","w":["That was quarantine in later plagues, not flagellants' penance.","Burning clothes was a precaution, not a religious act of penance.","","Fleeing to the countryside was what the rich did; flagellants walked between towns."]}
{"id":"ghi-003","s":"history","t":"ph-2","lv":1,"q":"During plague outbreaks in the 1600s, what happened to a house where someone had plague?","o":["It was burned down straight away","It was shut up with the family inside and marked with a red cross","It was given to the church","The family was moved to the countryside"],"a":1,"x":"Houses were shut up for forty days with a red cross on the door, guarded by watchmen. This protected others but was cruel to healthy people shut in with the sick.","w":["Infected houses were sealed and guarded, not burned down.","","No rule gave plague houses to the church.","Wealthy people fled; infected families were kept shut in."]}
{"id":"ghi-004","s":"history","t":"ph-2","lv":2,"q":"Why was the 1751 Gin Act more effective than the 1736 Gin Act?","o":["It stopped distillers selling to unlicensed sellers","It banned the making of all alcohol","It made gin free for the poor","It was enforced by the army"],"a":0,"x":"The 1736 Act's £50 licence was so high that only two were ever bought, so the trade went underground. The 1751 Act banned distillers selling to unlicensed shops, which could be enforced. Rising grain prices also cut gin drinking.","w":["","The Act only targeted unlicensed gin sales, not all alcohol.","The aim was to cut gin drinking, not give it away free.","Enforcement relied on magistrates and licensing, not the army."]}
{"id":"ghi-005","s":"history","t":"ph-3","lv":1,"q":"In 1854 John Snow linked a cholera outbreak in Soho to:","o":["A bad smell from the Thames","A shipment of foreign cotton","A water pump in Broad Street","Overcrowding in lodging houses"],"a":2,"x":"Snow mapped the deaths and found they clustered around the Broad Street pump. The handle was removed, though cases were already falling. His evidence supported his idea that cholera spread through dirty water.","w":["Mixes up miasma ideas with Snow's evidence; he blamed water, not smells.","Cotton had no link to the Soho cholera outbreak.","","Overcrowding was a real problem, but Snow's map pointed to one pump."]}
{"id":"ghi-006","s":"history","t":"ph-3","lv":2,"q":"What was a main weakness of the 1848 Public Health Act?","o":["Most of its measures were not compulsory for local councils","It only applied to London","It banned all new factories","It set up no Board of Health"],"a":0,"x":"The 1848 Act created a General Board of Health but local councils were mostly not forced to act unless the death rate was very high. Many ignored it because reform cost money.","w":["","It actually left London out; London was covered by separate laws.","It didn't ban factories; it was about water, sewers and health.","It did set up a General Board of Health."]}
{"id":"ghi-007","s":"history","t":"ph-4","lv":1,"q":"Which Act of 1956 followed the Great Smog of London in 1952?","o":["The Public Health Act","The Housing Act","The National Health Service Act","The Clean Air Act"],"a":3,"x":"The Great Smog of December 1952 was linked to thousands of deaths. The Clean Air Act 1956 created smokeless zones and reduced coal smoke in cities.","w":["Public Health Acts were 1848 and 1875, long before the 1952 smog.","The Housing Act dealt with slums, not smoke in the air.","The NHS Act was 1946 and was about healthcare, not pollution.",""]}
{"id":"ghi-008","s":"history","t":"ph-4","lv":2,"q":"What was the main aim of the government's 'Don't Die of Ignorance' AIDS campaign in 1986-87?","o":["To announce a vaccine had been found","To tell people how AIDS is spread and how to reduce the risk","To ban people with AIDS from public places","To raise money for blood testing only"],"a":1,"x":"There was no cure, so the government used leaflets sent to every home and TV adverts to spread information about how HIV was passed on and how to avoid catching it.","w":["No vaccine existed; that is why the campaign focused on prevention.","","The campaign gave advice; it didn't ban anyone from public places.","It was an information campaign, not a fundraiser."]}
{"id":"ghi-009","s":"history","t":"ph-5","lv":1,"q":"The creation of the NHS in 1948 is the best example of which factor in changing health?","o":["Religious belief","Chance discovery","Government action","Natural disasters"],"a":2,"x":"The NHS, set up by Clement Attlee's Labour government with Aneurin Bevan as health minister, gave free healthcare for all. It shows the role of government in improving public health.","w":["The NHS was a political decision, not a religious one.","It was planned policy from the Beveridge Report, not luck.","","Disasters didn't create the NHS; a government did."]}
{"id":"ghi-010","s":"history","t":"ph-5","lv":3,"q":"Why was Louis Pasteur's germ theory of the 1860s important for public health?","o":["It proved that bad air causes disease","It gave scientific evidence that microbes cause disease","It produced a cure for cholera","It led directly to the NHS"],"a":1,"x":"Pasteur showed that microbes cause disease, replacing the belief in miasma. This gave governments and scientists a scientific reason to provide clean water and sewers.","w":["Pasteur's theory disproved miasma; it didn't support bad air.","","Pasteur didn't cure cholera; Koch identified its microbe in 1883.","The NHS came in 1948, far later and for other reasons."]}
{"id":"ghi-011","s":"history","t":"el-1","lv":1,"q":"Who were the members of Elizabeth I's Privy Council?","o":["Advisers chosen by the queen to help her govern","MPs elected by landowners","Bishops chosen by the Pope","Judges who tried criminals"],"a":0,"x":"The Privy Council was a small group of nobles and officials, such as William Cecil, who advised Elizabeth and ran the government day to day. She chose its members.","w":["","That describes Parliament; the Privy Council was chosen by the queen.","Elizabeth was Supreme Governor of the Church; the Pope had no say.","Judges ran the courts; the Council advised on government."]}
{"id":"ghi-012","s":"history","t":"el-1","lv":3,"q":"Why did the Earl of Essex rebel against Elizabeth in February 1601?","o":["He wanted to make England Catholic again","He had lost favour and wanted to remove her advisers and win back influence","He had been paid by Spain to kill the queen","He wanted to end taxes on the poor"],"a":1,"x":"Essex had failed in Ireland and lost royal favour, and the queen refused to renew his sweet wine monopoly, leaving him in debt. He wanted to remove rivals such as Robert Cecil. He rose on 8 February and was executed on 25 February 1601.","w":["Essex was Protestant; this was a bid for power, not a religious rebellion.","","No evidence Spain paid him; he wanted to remove rivals at court.","He acted for his own influence, not to help the poor."]}
{"id":"ghi-013","s":"history","t":"el-2","lv":1,"q":"In which year was the Spanish Armada defeated?","o":["1558","1570","1588","1603"],"a":2,"x":"The Armada sailed in 1588 to invade England. English fireships, the weather and the Spanish being unable to join up with their army led to its defeat.","w":["Mixes it up with Elizabeth becoming queen in 1558, 30 years too early","1570 is when the Pope excommunicated Elizabeth, not the Armada","","1603 is the year Elizabeth died, long after the Armada"]}
{"id":"ghi-014","s":"history","t":"el-2","lv":3,"q":"What was the result of the Babington Plot of 1586 for Mary, Queen of Scots?","o":["She was freed and allowed to leave England","She was crowned queen of England","Her letters were found and she was executed in 1587","She was sent to live in France"],"a":2,"x":"Walsingham's spies intercepted Mary's letters approving a plot to kill Elizabeth. She was tried for treason and executed at Fotheringhay in February 1587.","w":["Opposite: the plot ended any hope of freedom for Mary","Mary wanted the crown, but the plot failed and she never got it","","She lived in France as a child, but in 1586 she was a prisoner in England"]}
{"id":"ghi-015","s":"history","t":"el-3","lv":1,"q":"In Elizabethan law, who were the 'impotent poor'?","o":["Healthy men who refused to work","People who could not work, such as the old, sick and disabled","Wandering players and actors","Landowners who could not pay taxes"],"a":1,"x":"The impotent poor were seen as 'deserving' because they could not work and were helped, for example in almshouses. Fit beggars, the 'sturdy beggars', could be punished.","w":["Confuses them with 'sturdy beggars', who were fit but idle and punished","","","'Impotent' means unable to work, not unable to pay taxes"]}
{"id":"ghi-016","s":"history","t":"el-3","lv":2,"q":"What did the 1601 Poor Law do?","o":["Made each parish collect a poor rate to help its poor","Gave all poor people free food from the queen","Ended all help for the old and sick","Sent the poor to colonies in America"],"a":0,"x":"The 1601 Act made parishes responsible for their own poor, funded by a compulsory poor rate on householders. It required overseers to provide work for the able and relief for the impotent poor.","w":["","Help came from local poor rates paid by householders, not from the queen","Opposite: the Act required relief for the old and sick","Colonies were not the answer; the poor were helped within their parish"]}
{"id":"ghi-017","s":"history","t":"el-4","lv":2,"q":"Why did Puritans criticise the theatres of Elizabethan England?","o":["They thought plays were too expensive","They thought plays were immoral and drew people away from church","They thought plays should only be about the queen","They thought actors were spies for Spain"],"a":1,"x":"Many Puritans said plays encouraged idleness and immorality. City authorities also worried about disorder and plague in crowded playhouses, and often tried to restrict them.","w":["Cost wasn't the Puritan complaint; they objected on moral grounds","","Made up; Puritans objected to the morals of plays, not who they were about","Mixes up fear of Spanish spies with the Puritan worry about immorality"]}
{"id":"ghi-018","s":"history","t":"el-4","lv":2,"q":"Who was most likely to be accused of witchcraft in Elizabethan England?","o":["A rich merchant in a big town","A young nobleman at court","A priest from the local church","A poor, elderly woman living alone who had quarrelled with neighbours"],"a":3,"x":"Accusations often fell on poor women, many widows, who depended on charity. If a neighbour then suffered bad luck, such as a sick child, they could be blamed.","w":["Rich merchants had status and protection, so were rarely accused","Nobles at court were powerful and seldom accused","Priests were respected figures; accusations targeted the poor and vulnerable",""]}
{"id":"ghi-019","s":"history","t":"el-5","lv":1,"q":"Which captain became the first Englishman to sail around the world, in 1577-80?","o":["Walter Raleigh","John Hawkins","Francis Drake","Martin Frobisher"],"a":2,"x":"Drake sailed the Golden Hind round the world and returned with Spanish treasure. Elizabeth knighted him in 1581.","w":["Raleigh is linked to Roanoke, not sailing round the world","Hawkins is known for slave-trading voyages, not circumnavigation","","Frobisher searched for the North-West Passage, not a round-the-world trip"]}
{"id":"ghi-020","s":"history","t":"el-5","lv":2,"q":"What did John White find when he returned to the Roanoke colony in 1590?","o":["The settlers had gone and the word 'Croatoan' was carved on a post","The colony had grown into a rich town","The settlers had been killed by Spanish soldiers","The settlers had sailed home to England"],"a":0,"x":"White had left in 1587 and was delayed by the Armada. In 1590 he found the houses empty and 'Croatoan' carved on a post, and the settlers were never found.","w":["","Roanoke failed; White found empty houses, not a thriving town","No evidence of Spanish attack; the carved word pointed elsewhere","No sign they went home; the settlers were never found"]}
{"id":"ghi-021","s":"history","t":"hau-1","lv":1,"q":"Why were many medieval castles built on hills or next to rivers?","o":["It made them cheaper to heat","The king only allowed castles there","The sites were easy to defend and gave a good view of the land","They were nearer to the church"],"a":2,"x":"A hill or river made attack harder and gave a good lookout. A historian studying a site asks why it is where it is, looking at its geography and who it was meant to control.","w":["Heating wasn't the reason; defence and lookout were","Kings didn't limit castles to such sites; builders chose them for defence","","Being near a church had no bearing on castle siting"]}
{"id":"ghi-022","s":"history","t":"hau-1","lv":2,"q":"Which would best show how a historic site changed over time?","o":["A guidebook from last year","A single photograph taken today","The opinion of one visitor","Plans, maps and paintings from different dates"],"a":3,"x":"Comparing sources from different dates shows what was added, changed or lost. A modern photograph shows only the site as it is now.","w":["A modern guidebook shows the site only as it is now","One photo is a snapshot of today and shows no change over time","One opinion can't show how a site changed over time",""]}
{"id":"ghi-023","s":"history","t":"hau-2","lv":2,"q":"When a historian asks how typical a site is, they are comparing it with:","o":["Other similar sites from the same period","Sites from completely different countries","Modern buildings in the same town","The first building ever built"],"a":0,"x":"To judge whether a site is typical, historians compare features such as layout, size and use with similar sites of the time. Unusual features show where the site is special.","w":["","Sites from other countries aren't like-for-like, so can't show how typical it is","Modern buildings are from a different period, so can't judge typicality","One very early building from another time can't show what was normal"]}
{"id":"ghi-024","s":"history","t":"hau-2","lv":2,"q":"Which source would best tell historians who used a building and for what purpose?","o":["The colour of the stone","Documents such as household accounts and inventories","A modern map of the car park","The number of visitors per year"],"a":1,"x":"Written records of the time can show who lived and worked at a site and what they did. Physical remains alone often cannot show how rooms were used.","w":["Stone colour says nothing about who used the building or why","","A modern car park map tells you nothing about past use","Visitor numbers show popularity today, not the original purpose"]}
{"id":"ghi-025","s":"history","t":"hau-3","lv":2,"q":"A reconstruction drawing of a ruined building is useful but should be treated carefully because it is:","o":["Based on evidence and informed guesses, so it is an interpretation","A photograph taken at the time","Always based on written plans","Created by the people who lived there"],"a":0,"x":"Reconstructions use surviving remains and documents, but gaps are filled by judgement, so different experts may draw different versions.","w":["","A reconstruction is drawn later, so it can't be a photograph from the time.","Plans rarely survive in full, so artists also use remains and guesses.","It is drawn by later artists or experts, not by the people who lived there."]}
{"id":"ghi-026","s":"history","t":"hau-3","lv":3,"q":"What is one limitation of using only the physical remains of a site as evidence?","o":["They always show the site as it was in its first year","Parts have decayed or been rebuilt, and they show little about people's thoughts","They cannot be visited by historians","They are written down by the owners"],"a":1,"x":"Remains survive unevenly and later changes may hide earlier ones. They show what was built and used, but not the beliefs and feelings of the people involved, so historians combine them with written sources.","w":["Remains are often altered or rebuilt, so they rarely show the first year.","","Historians can visit sites; the limits are decay and what remains reveal.","Remains are buildings and objects, not writing, so no owner wrote them down."]}
{"id":"ghi-027","s":"history","t":"ma-1","lv":1,"q":"Which country sold the Louisiana Territory to the USA in 1803?","o":["Spain","Britain","Mexico","France"],"a":3,"x":"Napoleon sold Louisiana to the USA in 1803 under President Jefferson, roughly doubling the size of the country.","w":["Spain once owned it but had handed it back to France before 1803.","Britain never owned Louisiana; France sold it.","Mexico was not yet independent in 1803 and did not own Louisiana.",""]}
{"id":"ghi-028","s":"history","t":"ma-1","lv":2,"q":"How did the invention of the cotton gin (1793) affect the South?","o":["It ended the need for enslaved workers","It made the North grow more cotton","It banned cotton farming in the South","Cotton became more profitable, increasing the demand for enslaved workers"],"a":3,"x":"Eli Whitney's gin cleaned cotton much faster. More cotton could be sold, so planters spread across the South and relied on more enslaved workers to grow it.","w":["Back to front: the gin made cotton more profitable, so more enslaved workers were needed.","The gin boosted cotton growing in the South, not the North.","Cotton farming expanded hugely in the South after the gin; it wasn't banned.",""]}
{"id":"ghi-029","s":"history","t":"ma-1","lv":2,"q":"What did the Indian Removal Act of 1830 allow the US government to do?","o":["Move Native Americans from the east to land west of the Mississippi","Give Native Americans full citizenship","Ban white settlers from Native land","Pay Native Americans for crops"],"a":0,"x":"Under President Andrew Jackson, Native peoples were forced west. The Cherokee removal in 1838-39 became known as the Trail of Tears, and many died on the journey.","w":["","Citizenship wasn't given; the Act enabled forced removal.","The opposite: it opened Native land to white settlement.","Nothing about paying for crops; the Act was about forced removal west."]}
{"id":"ghi-030","s":"history","t":"ma-2","lv":1,"q":"For Plains Indians, what was the most important use of the buffalo?","o":["It gave food, shelter, clothing and tools","It was kept only for trade with settlers","It was used only for religious ceremonies","It was hunted only for sport"],"a":0,"x":"Plains peoples used almost every part of the buffalo: meat for food, hides for tipis and clothing, and bones for tools. Its loss in the 1870s-80s was devastating.","w":["","Plains peoples used buffalo themselves, not just for trade with settlers.","Ceremonies mattered, but buffalo were used far more widely in daily life.","Plains peoples hunted buffalo to survive, not for sport."]}
{"id":"ghi-031","s":"history","t":"ma-2","lv":2,"q":"Why did the Mormons travel west to Utah in the 1840s?","o":["To search for gold in California","To join the US army","To escape persecution and build their own community","To trade with Native Americans"],"a":2,"x":"Mormons had been driven out of several states, and their leader Joseph Smith was killed in 1844. Brigham Young led them to the Great Salt Lake Valley in 1847.","w":["That was the 1849 Gold Rush; Mormons went to Utah seeking safety.","They weren't joining the army; they wanted freedom to practise their faith.","","Trade wasn't the aim; they fled persecution to build their own community."]}
{"id":"ghi-032","s":"history","t":"ma-3","lv":1,"q":"Which event in April 1861 began the American Civil War?","o":["The Battle of Gettysburg","Lee's surrender at Appomattox","The Confederate attack on Fort Sumter","The assassination of Lincoln"],"a":2,"x":"Southern forces fired on Fort Sumter in South Carolina after seven states had left the Union following Lincoln's election. Gettysburg was in 1863 and Appomattox in 1865.","w":["Gettysburg was in 1863, two years into the war.","Appomattox in 1865 ended the war rather than began it.","","Lincoln was assassinated in 1865, near the end of the war, not the start."]}
{"id":"ghi-033","s":"history","t":"ma-3","lv":3,"q":"Why did many freed African Americans become sharecroppers after the Civil War?","o":["They had no land or money, so they farmed another's land for a share of the crop","The government gave each family 40 acres","Northern law forced them to work on farms","They wanted to join the army"],"a":0,"x":"Most freed people were given no land. Sharecropping often kept them in debt to landowners, so economic freedom was limited even after slavery ended.","w":["","'40 acres and a mule' was briefly promised but mostly never delivered.","Northern law didn't force them; lack of land and money pushed them into it.","Joining the army wasn't the reason; lack of land and money was."]}
{"id":"ghi-034","s":"history","t":"ma-4","lv":1,"q":"Under the Homestead Act of 1862, what could settlers receive?","o":["A free railway ticket west","A loan to buy cattle","A job working for the army","160 acres of land if they farmed it for five years"],"a":3,"x":"The Homestead Act offered 160 acres to anyone who lived on and farmed the land for five years. Many settlers moved onto the Plains, and some struggled with drought and poor soil.","w":["Railway companies sold land cheaply, but the Act gave land, not tickets.","The Act gave land, not loans for cattle.","It offered farmland, not army jobs.",""]}
{"id":"ghi-035","s":"history","t":"ma-4","lv":2,"q":"Where were the Union Pacific and Central Pacific railroads joined in 1869?","o":["Chicago, Illinois","Omaha, Nebraska","Promontory Summit, Utah","Sacramento, California"],"a":2,"x":"The first transcontinental railroad was completed at Promontory Summit in Utah on 10 May 1869. Travel across the continent fell from months to about a week.","w":["Chicago was a rail hub, but far from where the two lines met.","Omaha was the Union Pacific's starting point, not where the lines joined.","","Sacramento was where the Central Pacific began, not where the lines met."]}
{"id":"ghi-036","s":"history","t":"ma-4","lv":3,"q":"Why did the discovery of gold in the Black Hills in 1874 lead to war with the Sioux?","o":["The railroad company demanded the land for its tracks","The railroad refused to carry the gold","Mormon settlers had already claimed it","Miners flooded in to land that the 1868 treaty had promised to the Sioux"],"a":3,"x":"The Black Hills were sacred to the Sioux and protected by the Fort Laramie Treaty of 1868. Fighting in 1876 included the defeat of Custer's troops at Little Bighorn.","w":["The railroad wasn't the cause; miners broke the treaty by entering Sioux land.","The issue was land and the treaty, not carrying gold.","Mormons settled in Utah, not the Black Hills; miners were the intruders.",""]}
{"id":"ghi-037","s":"history","t":"ma-5","lv":3,"q":"What was the aim of the Dawes Act of 1887?","o":["To give reservations independence","To return land taken by settlers","To stop buffalo hunting","To break up reservation land and make Native Americans into farmers"],"a":3,"x":"The Dawes Act divided reservation land into individual plots. It aimed to assimilate Native Americans, but it reduced the land they held and weakened tribal life.","w":["Opposite: it broke up reservations and cut tribal land, not gave independence.","Reversed: Native Americans lost land under the Act, they did not regain it.","Mixes it up with buffalo slaughter; the Act was about land ownership, not hunting.",""]}
{"id":"ghi-038","s":"history","t":"ma-5","lv":2,"q":"What was the effect of the Supreme Court's Plessy v Ferguson decision in 1896?","o":["It ended segregation in schools","It gave Black Americans the vote","It allowed segregation if facilities were 'separate but equal'","It banned the Ku Klux Klan"],"a":2,"x":"The ruling said that separate facilities for Black and white people were legal if they were equal. In practice they were rarely equal, and it supported Jim Crow laws until the 1950s.","w":["Mixes it up with Brown v Board (1954); Plessy allowed segregation, not ended it.","Voting rights were a separate issue; Plessy was about segregated facilities.","","Nothing to do with the Klan; the ruling backed segregation and Jim Crow."]}
{"id":"ghi-039","s":"history","t":"nz-1","lv":1,"q":"Which event in February 1933 did Hitler blame on the Communists?","o":["The Munich Putsch","The Night of the Long Knives","The Enabling Act","The Reichstag fire"],"a":3,"x":"The Reichstag building burned on 27 February 1933. Hitler used it to persuade Hindenburg to suspend civil rights and arrest Communists.","w":["The Munich Putsch was Hitler's failed 1923 coup, not 1933.","The Night of the Long Knives was June 1934, a purge of the SA.","The Enabling Act came after the fire, in March 1933; it wasn't blamed on Communists.",""]}
{"id":"ghi-040","s":"history","t":"nz-1","lv":3,"q":"What did the Enabling Act of March 1933 allow Hitler to do?","o":["Become president as well as chancellor","Ban all churches in Germany","Hold a new Reichstag election every year","Make laws without the Reichstag's approval for four years"],"a":3,"x":"The Enabling Act passed on 23 March 1933 after Communists were barred and other parties were intimidated. It gave Hitler's government legal power to rule by decree.","w":["He became Führer after Hindenburg died in 1934; the Act was about lawmaking.","The Act gave power to make laws; churches were pressured, not banned outright.","Opposite: it removed the Reichstag's role rather than adding elections.",""]}
{"id":"ghi-041","s":"history","t":"nz-2","lv":1,"q":"What was the Gestapo?","o":["The Nazi youth movement","The Nazi secret state police","The German army","The Nazi propaganda ministry"],"a":1,"x":"The Gestapo, led by Himmler and Heydrich, spied on and arrested opponents of the regime. Many arrests came from tip-offs by ordinary citizens.","w":["That describes the Hitler Youth; the Gestapo was the secret police.","","The army was the Wehrmacht; the Gestapo was a police force.","Propaganda was Goebbels' ministry; the Gestapo spied and arrested."]}
{"id":"ghi-042","s":"history","t":"nz-2","lv":2,"q":"Why did Pastor Martin Niemöller help form the Confessing Church in 1934?","o":["To support the Hitler Youth","To oppose Nazi control of the Protestant Church","To help Hitler win the 1934 vote","To escape from Germany"],"a":1,"x":"Niemöller opposed the pro-Nazi 'German Christians'. He was arrested in 1937 and spent years in concentration camps.","w":["Opposite: Niemöller opposed Nazi interference and didn't back the Hitler Youth.","","He was against Nazi control, so he wouldn't help Hitler win a vote.","He stayed in Germany and was arrested in 1937; his aim was to resist, not flee."]}
{"id":"ghi-043","s":"history","t":"nz-3","lv":1,"q":"What did the Nuremberg Laws of 1935 do?","o":["Forced Jews to leave Germany at once","Removed Jews' German citizenship and banned Jewish-'Aryan' marriage","Closed all Jewish shops","Created the Hitler Youth"],"a":1,"x":"The Nuremberg Laws defined who was Jewish and made them second-class citizens. They were a major step in the Nazis' persecution of Jews.","w":["Forced emigration came later; these laws removed rights but didn't expel Jews at once.","","Shop boycotts and Kristallnacht targeted businesses; the Nuremberg Laws did not.","The Hitler Youth was founded in the 1920s; it is unrelated to these laws."]}
{"id":"ghi-044","s":"history","t":"nz-3","lv":2,"q":"What did the Nazis use as an excuse for the Kristallnacht attacks in November 1938?","o":["A Jewish attack on the Reichstag","The murder of a German diplomat in Paris by a Jewish teenager","The failure of a Jewish business","A Jewish plot against Hitler's life"],"a":1,"x":"Herschel Grynszpan shot Ernst vom Rath. The Nazis organised attacks on synagogues and Jewish shops on 9-10 November, and thousands of Jewish men were arrested.","w":["Mixes it up with the 1933 Reichstag fire, which was blamed on Communists.","","Invented; the excuse was a shooting in Paris, not a business failure.","The 1944 bomb plot was by army officers, not Jews; wrong event."]}
{"id":"ghi-045","s":"history","t":"nz-4","lv":3,"q":"Why did Goebbels call for 'total war' in February 1943?","o":["After Stalingrad, he wanted all people and resources put into the war","Germany had just won at Stalingrad","Britain had invaded Germany","The Nazis wanted to end the war"],"a":0,"x":"Defeat at Stalingrad shocked Germany. In his Sportpalast speech Goebbels called on the nation to mobilise everyone and everything for war, including women and the closure of non-essential businesses.","w":["","Germany lost at Stalingrad; that defeat is why total war was called.","Britain never invaded Germany in 1943; the trigger was defeat in the USSR.","Total war meant fighting harder, not ending the war."]}
{"id":"ghi-046","s":"history","t":"nz-4","lv":2,"q":"What happened to the 20 July 1944 bomb plot against Hitler?","o":["The bomb killed Hitler and the war ended","The bomb failed to go off and the plot was never discovered","The bomb exploded but Hitler survived and the plotters were punished","The army took over the government"],"a":2,"x":"Claus von Stauffenberg placed a bomb at Hitler's headquarters, but Hitler survived. Stauffenberg was shot and many others were executed.","w":["Hitler survived and the war carried on until 1945.","The bomb did explode, and the plotters were quickly found and executed.","","The army did not take power; the coup failed and the Nazis stayed in control."]}
{"id":"ghi-047","s":"history","t":"nz-5","lv":2,"q":"What was the main purpose of the Wannsee Conference in January 1942?","o":["To coordinate the Nazi plan to murder Europe's Jews","To plan the invasion of Britain","To end the war with the Soviet Union","To decide where Jews could settle in Germany"],"a":0,"x":"Senior Nazis, led by Reinhard Heydrich, met in Berlin to organise the 'Final Solution' across Europe. Mass murder by the Einsatzgruppen had already begun.","w":["","The invasion of Britain was a 1940 plan; Wannsee was about the Final Solution.","Wannsee was about Jews, not peace; the war with the USSR went on.","Settling Jews in Germany wasn't the plan; it was murder across Europe."]}
{"id":"ghi-048","s":"history","t":"nz-5","lv":1,"q":"Which marshal led the collaborationist government in southern France after 1940?","o":["Charles de Gaulle","Ferdinand Foch","Joseph Joffre","Philippe Pétain"],"a":3,"x":"After France's defeat in 1940, the Vichy government under Pétain cooperated with Germany. De Gaulle led the Free French in exile.","w":["De Gaulle led the Free French in exile, opposing Vichy.","Foch was a First World War Allied commander who died in 1929.","Joffre was a First World War French commander who died in 1931.",""]}
